  "passage_locator": {
    "label": "MIRTS-TZ, AND HIS SON ZOHK / KAVAH, THE BLACKSMITH / FERIDN / FERIDN AND HIS THREE SONS; lines 1868-1964",
    "start": "1868",
    "end": "1964",
    "translation": "Persian Literature, Volume 1",
    "notes": "Stable markdown line range generated from canonical text."
  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Feridn has three sons, Slim, Tr, and Irij. He sends a learned man, Chundel, to seek three suitable daughters from one family to marry them. Chundel eventually reaches Sar, king of Yemen, who has three accomplished daughters and agrees to the proposal; Feridn's sons marry them and receive rich dowries. Feridn then divides his empire among his sons: Slim receives Rm and Khwer, Tr receives Trn, and Irij receives Irn (Persia). Persia is described as beautiful and flourishing, whereas Trn is rough and troubled. Slim, the eldest, becomes discontented with this division and complains to Tr that Irij has the best kingdom while they have wild regions. Tr agrees and suggests they first inform their father of their dissatisfaction and seek a redistribution. A messenger is sent to Feridn bearing an ultimatum that unless Irij is given a more humble portion, Slim and Tr will raise armies from their territories and bring ruin on Irij and Persia. The messenger appears before Feridn, who replies that he has done them no wrong, reminds them to fear God, and counsels them to be content with their allotted kingdoms and to cherish peace. After the messenger leaves, Feridn summons Irij and tells him of his elder brothers' alliance and their threat to attack him, and possibly Feridn himself if he supports Irij. Feridn, now old and wishing for retirement, advises Irij that he cannot withstand their forces and should renounce ambition, pursue peace, and relinquish his dominion rather than go to war. Irij agrees, saying he will willingly sacrifice his throne rather than fight his brothers, reflecting on the brevity of human life and the illusory nature of worldly glory, and resolves to go to his brothers and place his kingdom at their disposal in the hope of winning their favor.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Feridn has three sons named Slim, Tr, and Irij.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Feridn instructs a learned person named Chundel to seek three daughters of the same parents, adorned with grace and accomplishments, as wives for his three sons.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Chundel travels through many countries in search of suitable daughters.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Chundel arrives at the court of Sar, king of Yemen, who has three daughters fitting Feridn's requirements, and delivers Feridn's marriage proposal, which Sar accepts.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Feridn sends his three sons to Yemen, where they marry the three daughters of the king of Yemen and receive splendid dowries of treasure and jewels.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Feridn divides his empire among his sons, giving Slim Rm and Khwer, Tr the land of Trn, and Irij Irn or Persia.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Persia is described as a beautiful, garden-like country full of freshness and perfume, while Trn is described as less cultivated and plagued by broils and insurrections.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Slim is discontented with the partition of the empire and displeased with his father.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Slim sends a message to Tr complaining that their father has given Irij the most delightful and productive kingdom while leaving them wild, uncultivated regions.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Tr agrees with Slim's complaint and proposes that they first inform their father of their dissatisfaction, hoping that in a new distribution Persia might be assigned to him.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Tr writes to Slim proposing that a messenger be sent to Feridn to convey their grievances and bring back a reply, and Slim dispatches such a messenger.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "The messenger bears an ultimatum stating that unless Irij is given a more humble portion, Slim and Tr will raise overwhelming troops from the mountains of Trn, and from Rm and Chn, to shower disgrace and ruin on Irij and Persia.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4",
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Upon arriving at Feridn's court, the messenger kisses the ground respectfully and, by command, states the purpose of his journey.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Feridn responds that he has done them no wrong by giving them kingdoms, reminds them to fear God, notes his approaching old age and the fleeting nature of worldly possessions, and counsels them with paternal love to be content with their lot and avoid unnatural strife, cherishing peace instead.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "After the messenger departs, Feridn calls Irij and informs him that his two older brothers have allied and threaten to bring a large army against him to seize his kingdom and kill him, and that they will wage war on Feridn himself if he takes Irij's part.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Feridn tells Irij that he is unable to resist even one brother's invasion, and advises him to abandon desire for dominion, pursue peace and friendship, and, like Feridn, throw away all desire for worldly rule in favor of retirement and repose.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "Feridn warns Irij that if the sword of anger is unsheathed and war begins, his head will soon be freed from all the cares of government and life.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "Irij agrees with his father's counsel and declares that he will willingly sacrifice his throne and diadem rather than go to war with his brothers.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "Irij reflects on the Heavens and the brevity of human life, describing man as soon gone like a breath of wind, and questions whether such a world should be deplored.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "Irij resolves to repair immediately to his brothers, place his kingdom at their disposal, and hope thereby to calm their anger and gain their favor, while acknowledging that vengeance might seem more fitting than offers of peace.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    }
  ],
