      "quote_or_summary": "The messenger arrives at Feridn's court, kisses the ground respectfully, and, by command, states the purpose of his journey. Feridn is surprised and displeased, and replies that he has done no wrong, only good, by giving his sons kingdoms. He urges them, if they do not fear him, at least to fear God, notes that his life is ebbing and that worldly possessions will soon pass from him, and says he is too old to have his passions roused by rebellion. He offers paternal counsel for peace, telling them to be content with their lot, avoid unnatural strife, and cherish peace.",
      "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/persian/project-gutenberg/persian-literature-volume-1.md",
