  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Rustem is settled in Sistan by Zl, where a white elephant belonging to Minchihr breaks loose at night and tramples people. Ignoring servants' warnings, Rustem kills a watchman who hinders him, breaks through the gate, confronts the enormous elephant described as like a mountain and roaring like the Nile, and slays it with a single blow, earning Zl’s astonished praise. Zl then decides Rustem should avenge his grandfather Narmn, who was sent by Feridn against an enchanted fort on the mountain Sipund and killed by a rock thrown from above. The fort is many miles high, full of verdant lawns, gardens, and treasure, and is surrounded by trackless desert; Sm had earlier attempted and failed to take it. Following Zl’s plan and his father’s advice, Rustem disguises himself as a salt-merchant, hides weapons among the salt on a caravan of camels, and gains entry when the garrison eagerly opens the gates to buy the precious salt. At night he leads his warriors to attack the chief’s mansion; the Kotwl, awakened by the unusual noise, is killed by Rustem’s mace. A fierce nocturnal battle ensues in which Rustem and his companions slaughter the defenders, and at dawn Rustem kills the chief so that no inhabitants remain within the fort except fugitives. Rustem then breaks into the governor’s stone mansion with its iron gate and discovers a temple built with skill beyond mortal power and filled with immense wealth of jewels and gold. After the warriors gather as much treasure as they can carry, Rustem writes to Zl, who rejoices that Rustem has illuminated Narmn’s soul in Paradise by punishing his foes with fire and sword. Zl orders him to load the camels with treasure, burn and utterly destroy the fort leaving no trace, and Rustem obeys. Returning to Zabulistan, Rustem is embraced and publicly honored by Zl, warmly welcomed by his mother, and blessed by all.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
