      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The white elephant is described as looking like a mountain and roaring like the River Nil.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Rustem faces the elephant, shouts, and hits it with such force that his iron mace bends almost double, causing the elephant to tremble and fall dead.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "When Zl hears that Rustem killed the elephant with one blow, he is amazed, thanks heaven, and calls Rustem his darling boy, unequalled in valor and magnanimity.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Zl decides that, after such an achievement, Rustem would be suitable to take vengeance on the enemies of his grandfather Narmn.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Narmn was sent by Feridn with a large army against an enchanted fort on the mountain Sipund and was killed by a piece of rock thrown down from above by the besieged.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "The enchanted fort is many miles high, enclosing verdant lawns and delightful gardens with fruit and flowers, and is full of treasure.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "When Sm hears of his father Narmn’s death, he is deeply afflicted and later marches against the fort but is blocked by a trackless desert and by gates through which no beings are ever seen to pass; after months and years he gives up the attempt in despair.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Zl tells Rustem that the time and remedy have come and that, being yet unknown, Rustem can easily accomplish their purpose.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "Rustem agrees to the adventure and, following his father’s advice, assumes the dress and character of a salt-merchant, prepares a caravan of camels, and hides arms among the loads of salt.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Upon reaching the fort on the mountain Sipund, Rustem’s caravan offers salt, a precious and much-needed commodity, so the garrison opens the gates and men, women, and children crowd around buying salt in exchange for clothes, gold, and silver, without suspicion of Rustem and his warriors.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "At night Rustem impatiently leads his warriors toward the chief’s mansion; the unusual noise of the armed movement awakens the Kotwl, who springs up to confront the danger, but Rustem raises his heavy mace, splits the Kotwl’s head, and scatters his brains on the ground, as the garrison hurries to the battle scene where swords flash amidst deep gloom.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "Rustem fights the people of the fort all night and, at dawn, finds and kills the chief; the survivors flee, and no inhabitants remain alive within the fort’s walls.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "Rustem then goes to the governor’s stone mansion, bursts open its iron gate with his battle-axe, and discovers a temple inside, constructed with skill beyond mortal power and containing astonishing wealth in jewels and gold.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "Rustem’s warriors gather as much treasure as they can carry, and Rustem writes to Zl to ask for further commands concerning the capture.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "Zl, overjoyed at the success, replies that Rustem has illuminated Narmn’s soul, now in the blissful bowers of Paradise, by punishing his foes with fire and sword.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:7"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:21",
      "text": "Zl orders Rustem to load the camels with as much of the valuable property as can be carried away and then to burn and destroy the entire place, leaving not a single trace; Rustem obeys this command.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:7"]
    },
