    "summary": "Garshsp, still a minor and inexperienced in government, relies entirely on the counsel of Zl. In Trn, Afrsiyb's father Poshang is in deep grief and anger over Afrsiyb's inhuman murder of his innocent brother Aghrras, refuses to see him, and disowns him. Later, when Poshang hears that Garshsp is unequal to ruling Persia, he forgives Afrsiyb's crime for strategic reasons, gathers an immense army, and sends him to conquer Irn under the pretext of avenging the deaths of Slim and Tr. Afrsiyb's army crosses plains, rivers, mountains, and deserts, filling Persia with dread, and the chief authorities appeal to Zl as their only remedy. Zl announces that he has appointed his young son Rustem to command the army and oppose Afrsiyb. Zl describes Rustem as strong as an elephant yet still an infant whose lips smell of milk and whose heart seeks domestic joys, and he hesitates to send him to war, but Rustem replies that he rejects domestic pleasures and is fitted for other pursuits. Zl has trained Rustem thoroughly in war, finds his aptitude and skill surpassing his own, thanks God for this comfort, and Rustem takes up the huge mace of the champion Sm, which proves exactly suited to him. Seeking a matching steed, Rustem tests many horses but finds none strong enough, until he notices a mare with a remarkable foal, Rakush, whose dappled skin is likened to rose blossoms on a saffron lawn. A groom advises him to secure the foal, explaining that Rakush is offspring of Abresh, born of a Dw (demon), and that the dam has killed several people who tried to seize it. Rustem throws a noose and captures Rakush; the enraged mare attacks, but his loud cry makes her pause, and he strikes her head and neck, killing her. Rakush drags and pulls Rustem for some time before being subdued, and Rustem thanks Heaven for obtaining the horse he wanted. Declaring himself prepared with his horse to join the field of warriors, Rustem saddles Rakush; Zl watches with delight, his withered heart renewed, and opens his treasury in joy. Soon Zl sends Rustem with a prodigious army against Afrsiyb and joins him two days later. Afrsiyb remarks that the son is a boy and the father old and expects no difficulty in recovering Persia. Hearing this, Zl reflects that Garshsp cannot contend with Afrsiyb and that no other known prince of the race of Feridn exists, so he sends messengers in all directions. They learn that Kai-kobd, of the Kainian race, distinguished for wisdom and valor and fit for sovereign power, is living in obscurity on the mountain Alberz. Zl instructs Rustem to go forth quickly to Alberz and bring Kai-kobd from concealment, telling him that the army is prepared, the throne is ready, and that Kai-kobd alone of the Kainian race is deemed fit for sovereign rule.",
