    "summary": "Feridn, pleased that his younger son Irij seeks reconciliation while his elder sons Slim and Tr prepare for war, writes a letter urging the elders to cast away hostility, be kind to their younger brother, and remember their duty as eldest-born. Irij then travels discreetly with companions to Turkistn to deliver the letter; Slim and Tr, whose armies stand ready to march against him, receive him courteously in the royal residence. Irij's handsome appearance and bearing win the admiration of the troops and crowds, who consider him fit to be king and worthy of the throne and diadem. Courtiers, alarmed at the army's attachment to Irij, warn Slim and Tr that their soldiers favour him as rightful ruler. The brothers fear losing power and decide that Irij must be removed, speaking of uprooting a flourishing tree. Slim urges Tr to kill Irij so that his kingdom will be Tr's. Tr confronts Irij, accusing him of taking Persia without regard for his elder brothers, complaining that he enjoys peace and prosperity in a fertile land while they rule barren, war-torn territories and remain subordinate. Irij replies that he seeks only peace, renounces the throne and diadem, and reflects on the dark end of earthly pomp, declaring he will not contend against his brothers or distress any being. Tr disbelieves him, suddenly assaults him with a golden chair and has him bound. On the ground, Irij pleads for his life, invoking their father, Tr's own soul, and the vengeance of the Almighty, and in verse he urges compassion even for a harmless ant, warning against shedding a brother's blood and provoking the wrath of righteous Heaven. Unmoved, Tr beheads Irij with a dagger, embalms the severed head with musk and ambergris, and sends it to their old father with a cruel message inviting him to crown it and place it upon the throne as the fruit of his parenting.",
    "language": "English",
