    "summary": "Guthrun recalls weaving golden tapestries that depicted halls, swans, and the deeds and voyages of warriors. Grimhild, the Gothic queen, sets aside her needlework and calls her sons to make amends for Guthrun's slain husband, arranging the visit of foreign princes who offer gifts and comfort, which Guthrun distrusts. Grimhild then brews and gives Guthrun a bitter magical draught filled with earth, sea, animal parts, plants, and inscribed runes, causing Guthrun to forget her grief; Grimhild and kneeling kings offer wealth, halls, lands, and skilled weavers, trying to persuade Guthrun to marry Atli. Guthrun resists, mourning Sigurth's death and foretelling Atli's future killing of her brothers Gunnar and Hogni, while Grimhild weeps yet presses gifts and lands until Guthrun agrees to wed against her will, travels with heroes and foreign women through cold lands, sea, and dry regions, and arrives at a fortified hall. Later, Atli wakes Guthrun and recounts ominous dreams sent by the Norns: Guthrun stabbing him, bloody uprooted garden plants he is forced to eat, and hawks whose hearts soaked in blood and honey he consumes, while Guthrun answers darkly, speaking of fire, wrath, and healing.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Guthrun describes herself working gold embroidery and weaving tapestries showing southern halls and Danish swans.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The tapestry Guthrun weaves portrays warriors' deeds, heroes' thanes, and scenes of flashing shields and armed fighters.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Guthrun's weaving includes Sigmund's ship sailing by land, with a golden figurehead and ornate beaks.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The woven scene shows warriors Sigar and Siggeir traveling south to Fjon aboard Sigmund's ship.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Grimhild is engaged in needlework before she casts it aside to act.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Grimhild calls her sons and asks who will make amends to their sister for her dead son or compensate the widow for her slain husband.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Gunnar and Hogni declare themselves ready to give gold as amends for Guthrun's hurt.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Preparations for travel are listed: saddling a horse, harnessing a wagon, riding a horse, letting a hawk fly, and shooting arrows from yew bows.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Valdar, king of the Danes, arrives with Jarizleif, Eymoth, and Jarizskar; they are described as long-bearded men in red mantles, short mail-coats, mighty helms, with swords at their belts and brown hair.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The visiting princes each offer Guthrun gifts and fair words in an attempt to comfort her great sorrows, but she does not trust them.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Grimhild gives Guthrun a bitter, cold draught to drink which causes her to forget her cares.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "The drink is said to contain magic earth, ice-cold sea, swine's blood, runes of many kinds written and reddened, a heather-fish, an uncut ear, entrails of beasts, blossoms of trees, burned acorns, hearth dew, holy entrails, and a swine's liver.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Guthrun cannot read the runes that are inscribed and reddened in the cup.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "The beer is described as having much evil brewed in it and is explicitly said to be made to allay grief.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "After drinking the draught, Guthrun forgets her husband's slaying while she is in the hall.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Three kings kneel on their knees before Guthrun before Grimhild begins to speak.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "Grimhild offers Guthrun gold, her father's former wealth, rings, Hlothver's halls, hangings, Hunnish women skilled in weaving to bring joy, and the wealth of Buthli, promising she will be richly adorned as Atli's wife.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "Guthrun declares that she will not take a husband and refuses to be the wife of Brynhild's brother and the son of Buthli, saying it does not befit her to be happy with him or bear heirs.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "Grimhild advises Guthrun not to seek vengeance upon men despite the family's initial guilt and promises that bearing sons will make her as happy as if Sigurth and Sigmund still lived.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "Guthrun says she cannot find gladness or hope among heroes because ravens and a ravening wolf lapped Sigurth's heart's blood.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:21",
      "text": "Guthrun foresees that Atli's wrath will fall first on Gunnar and that he will tear the heart from Hogni's breast.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:22",
      "text": "Grimhild weeps on hearing Guthrun's words foretelling painful fate for her sons and offers Guthrun lands, including Vinbjorg and Valbjorg and all who live there, urging her daughter to accept them.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:23",
      "text": "Guthrun states that she must act as the kings request and wed against her will for the sake of her kinsmen, noting that she never had joy with her husband and her sons were not saved from her brothers' fate.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:24",
      "text": "Guthrun says she could not rest until she had deprived a bold warrior and battle-maker of life.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:25",
      "text": "Heroes ride on horseback and foreign women travel in wagons for a week through cold lands, a second week across waves at sea, and a third week through lands lacking water, until warders on lofty walls open the gates and the company rides in.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:26",
      "text": "Atli wakes Guthrun, and she is described as seeming continually full of bitterness over her brothers' deaths.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:27",
      "text": "Atli says the Norns have awakened him from sleep with terrifying visions and recounts a dream in which Guthrun pierced his body with a poisoned blade.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:28",
      "text": "Guthrun responds that an actual event of steel will follow the dream of steel and that, despite hatred, she will burn a baneful sore from Atli and tend and heal him.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:29",
      "text": "Atli describes a dream of garden plants drooping that he wished to grow high, which were plucked up by the roots, red with blood, brought to him, and he was told to eat them.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:30",
      "text": "Atli dreams his hawks fly from his hand to an evil house, and he eats their hearts with honey soaked in blood, which fills him with heavy sorrow.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:15"
      ]
    }
  ],
