  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Prn (Piran) seeks counsel from the Khakn, who reassures him that Rustem is a mortal opponent and authorizes him to negotiate peace. Prn then visits Rustem in the Persian camp; Rustem identifies himself and conveys blessings from Kai-khosru and his mother Ferangs. Prn blesses them in return, recalls that Saiwush, his daughter Glshaher’s husband, and Saiwush’s son Ferd and the boy’s mother were slain, and questions whether the guilty have been punished. He recounts how he secretly protected princess Ferangs and her son Kai-khosru from Afrsiyb, guarding them in his own house until they escaped across a boundary-stream, and urges peace since the current confederates are not guilty of Saiwush’s blood. Rustem replies that peace is impossible unless the principal agents of Saiwush’s death, especially Gerswaz, are surrendered and large sums are paid to the king of kings, and he negotiates only to serve Kai-khosru. Prn relays these terms to the Khakn and his confederates, who reject them out of pride and shame. Shinkul, king of Ind, vehemently opposes peace and, along with other warriors, proposes to overpower Rustem through successive attacks. Shinkul engages Rustem, is quickly defeated and flees, later describing Rustem to the Khakn as a more-than-mortal foe, like a furious elephant, a mountain, and a dragon. Granted five thousand men, Shinkul renews the battle; Rustem, now supported by his companions, fights a general engagement in which the dust darkens the sky and blood stains the plain. During the melee, Swa, a relation of Kms, attacks Rustem to avenge his friend, but is slain, as is another kinsman, Kahr, humbling the Kushanians’ pride. Elated, Rustem vows to confront the Khakn himself, assembles a thousand horsemen, assaults the kulub-gah (the Khakn’s headquarters), and amid heavy slaughter calls out to the Khakn.",
