    "summary": "In a devotional prelude, Firdusi invokes a single transcendent God as the 'Lord of Life and Light', describing Him as beyond imagination, all-encompassing, Creator and Guardian of the world, and the source of goodness, truth, and mercy. The poet asks for pardon for his misdeeds and prays that God will guide people to follow the path of His Prophet, cleanse their hearts from sin, and lead them gently to a 'home of rest' where the blessed dwell in holy rapture. The poem then refers to a divinely supplied book affirming Mustafa (Mahommed) as the true and unerring guide and greatest Prophet, followed by Abu Buker, Omer, Othmn, and Ali, who taught and spread the faith. Ali is described as the beloved spouse of Fatima and is praised with a saying of Mahommed: 'I am the city of knowledge—he my gate.' The poem promises that anyone who supplicates at Ali's all-powerful shrine will enjoy earthly good in this life and eternal bliss in the next. Firdusi turns to his own work, saying he recounts legends from true records and strings 'pearls of wisdom' into verse so that in his declining days he may reap its fruits in wealth and honor. He imagines his verse as a solid structure pointing to the skies that defies destructive time, asserts that all but the impious and malicious praise his work, and reports that the sovereign values it as richer than a mine of gems. He predicts that his pages will be the boast of future ages. The closing section is a panegyric to Mahmud, portrayed as equally renowned in battle and banquet, lord of the realms of China, Hindustan, Persia, and Turan, fierce as a tiger on land and like a crocodile on the waters of the Nile, deadly as a dragon's tooth yet generous and brave, with no equal in princely deeds. The infant in the cradle is said to lisp his name, and the world exults in his spotless fame. Mahmud is described as bounteous, diffusing blessings through the land, and as a ruler whose words carry the soul of Gabriel. The poet prays Heaven to crown Mahmud's days with added glory and offers him everlasting praise.",
