    "summary": "Editorial notes on stanzas 42–49 of the fragmentary short Voluspo discuss: the genealogy of Fenrir, Mithgarthsorm, Hel, and Sleipnir as children of Loki (often with the giantess Angrbotha); an obscure myth where Loki eats the cooked heart of a woman and then bears a monster; a referenced description of the destruction of the world; a possible reference to Christianity in Voluspo 65; and the continuation of the dialogue between Freyja and Hyndla in which Freyja identifies her boar as Ottar, who will use memory-beer to recall genealogies and win a wager with Angantyr. The notes also explain Heithrun, the she-goat at Valhall, as a pointed comment on Freyja’s morals (a view Loki shares), mention Oth, and suggest that lost lines probably stated Freyja would burn a giantess alive if she failed to bring the memory-beer.",
