    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "An obscure myth is mentioned in which Loki (Lopt) eats the cooked heart of a woman.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "As a result of eating the cooked heart, Loki himself gives birth to a monster.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The editor states that this monster is probably not the serpent, citing Snorri’s statement that the wolf, the serpent, and Hel were all children of Loki and Angrbotha.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "A description of the destruction of the world is said to be found in Voluspo stanza 57.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Voluspo stanza 65 is noted as possibly containing a reference to Christianity.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "With stanza 45 the fragmentary short Voluspo ends, and the dialogue between Freyja and Hyndla continues.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Freyja admits the identity of her boar as Ottar.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Ottar, with the help of memory-beer, is to recall the entire genealogy he has just heard and thereby win his wager with Angantyr.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Heithrun is identified as the she-goat that stands by Valhall, and the name is here used for she-goats in general.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "The mention of Heithrun is described as a caustic comment on Freyja’s morals, and Loki is said to entertain a similar view of her.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "The manuscript apparently repeats lines from stanza 47 in stanza 48, likely by mistake.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "The editor suggests that two lost lines probably stated that Freyja would burn a giantess alive if she did not quickly seek and bring the memory-beer.",
